  blurb:"A central availability + reservation service that many booking channels (Booking, Expedia, direct) all sell from. The crux is date-range correctness: a stay spans several nights, every night must have a room, and two channels racing for the last room on an overlapping range must never both win.",
  prompt:"Let's design a hotel reservation system. There is a central service that knows, for every hotel, how many rooms of each type are free on each night. Many booking channels — Booking.com, Expedia, the hotel's own site — all call it to sell the same physical rooms. I want two things to be bullet-proof. First, <em>never double-book</em>: if there is one room left for a Fri–Sun stay, exactly one guest gets it even if fifty requests land in the same millisecond across different channels. Second, keep the channels <em>consistent</em>: when one channel books a room, the others must stop selling it. Walk me through availability search over a date range, the reserve-then-confirm path, how channels push their bookings back, the collision cases, and how you'd make it extensible — new channels, overbooking policies, cancellations. Start with the high-level architecture and rough numbers, then we'll drill in and I'll throw concurrency at you.",
  opening:"Let me frame it before drawing boxes.<br><br><strong>Functional:</strong> <strong>search</strong> — for a hotel (or a city), a room type, and a stay <code>[checkin, checkout)</code>, tell me if <em>every</em> night has a room and what the total rate is; <strong>reserve</strong> — place a short-lived <strong>hold</strong> on a room for that date range, then <strong>confirm</strong> it into a booking; <strong>never double-book</strong> the same physical room; support <strong>cancellations / modifications</strong> that release inventory; and let <strong>many channels</strong> sell the same pooled inventory without overselling each other.<br><br><strong>The one insight that shapes everything:</strong> a hotel stay is an <strong>interval</strong>, not a single item. A Fri→Mon booking consumes <em>three</em> distinct nights (Fri, Sat, Sun). Availability is tracked <strong>per night</strong>, and a reserve must atomically confirm a room on <em>all</em> nights in the range or fail as a whole — it's an all-or-nothing decrement across a set of rows, which is what makes the concurrency interesting.<br><br><strong>Non-functional:</strong> the hard invariant is <strong>no oversell</strong> — on the write path correctness beats availability; I'll serialize the last-room race with row locks in a strongly-consistent store. Reserves are <strong>idempotent</strong> so a channel that retries a timed-out request doesn't book twice. <strong>Search</strong> is the opposite: ~100× the write volume, must be fast (p99 &lt; ~100ms) and highly available, and can tolerate slightly stale counts. Everything must be <strong>extensible</strong>: adding a channel, a room type, or an overbooking policy shouldn't need schema surgery.<br><br><strong>Back-of-envelope:</strong> ~<strong>1M</strong> hotels × ~<strong>10</strong> room types × a ~<strong>500-night</strong> booking horizon ≈ a few <strong>billion</strong> per-night rows — small bytes, easily sharded by <code>hotel_id</code>. Search peaks ~<strong>100K–1M/s</strong> (metasearch fans out across channels); reserves ~<strong>10K/s</strong> steady with sharp spikes when a concert or sale opens inventory in one city. Storage is trivial; the pressure is <strong>concurrency on a few hot (hotel, room_type, date) rows</strong> and <strong>keeping channels in sync</strong>.<br><br>I'll start deliberately minimal: <strong>channel → reservation API → availability store</strong>, where the store holds a decrementable room count per <code>(hotel, room_type, night)</code>. That skeleton already prevents oversell for a single night. Under pressure I'll grow it: a <strong>hold service</strong> for two-phase reserve→confirm, a <strong>booking store</strong> as system of record, an <strong>ARI cache</strong> for the search firehose, a <strong>channel manager</strong> that fans availability out and ingests channel bookings, and a <strong>reaper</strong> that expires abandoned holds. Pick a box and let's push.",
