Object.assign(window.DATA['hotel'].q, {
  ari:[
    {l:"easy",tag:"concept",q:"What is the ARI cache and why do you need it?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"Another cache — justify it."},
        {who:"cand",text:"ARI = <strong>Availability, Rates, Inventory</strong>. It's a read model (Redis / search index) keyed per <code>(hotel, room_type, date)</code> that fronts the availability store for the search firehose — the whole point is to absorb ~100x read load so the primary only handles writes and confirmations. It's disposable and eventually consistent; it is never the authority for a reserve."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"medium",tag:"consistency",q:"Two paths feed the cache — CDC from the store and event fan-out. How do you stop an out-of-order write from resurrecting a sold room?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"CDC and the channel-manager fan-out can arrive in any order. One could overwrite the other with stale data."},
        {who:"cand",text:"Every availability change carries a monotonic <strong>version</strong> (the row's <code>version</code> column). Cache writes are <strong>write-if-newer</strong>: apply only if the incoming version exceeds the stored one. So a late CDC message carrying an older version is dropped instead of flipping a room back to available. Both paths are idempotent and order-independent under that rule."},
        {who:"intv",text:"Belt and suspenders — why keep both paths at all?"},
        {who:"cand",text:"Fan-out gives sub-second cross-channel convergence; CDC guarantees the cache eventually equals the store even if an event is missed. A periodic reconciliation re-derives counts from the store to heal any drift. Redundant paths, reconciled by versioning."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"hard",tag:"consistency",q:"If the ARI cache is wrong, can it ever cause an actual oversell? Argue it precisely.",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"Convince me a bad cache can't oversell a room."},
        {who:"cand",text:"It can't, because the cache is strictly outside the write path. A reserve never trusts ARI; it executes the conditional decrement against the <strong>availability store</strong>, which is the only place <code>booked</code> changes and the only place the last-room race is arbitrated. A stale cache can only make search <em>optimistic</em> (show a room that's gone) or <em>pessimistic</em> (hide a room that exists)."},
        {who:"cand",text:"Optimistic staleness ends in a clean <code>409</code> at reserve — a display miss, not an oversell. Pessimistic staleness costs a lost sale until the cache heals. Neither can cause two guests to hold the same room, because that decision is made transactionally in the store, not the cache. The cache's correctness is a UX concern, not a safety one."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
  ],
  reaper:[
    {l:"easy",tag:"concept",q:"Why do you need a reaper at all?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"What goes wrong without it?"},
        {who:"cand",text:"Roughly half of holds never confirm — guests abandon checkout. Each hold decremented real rooms. Without a sweeper those rooms stay <code>booked</code> forever, so the hotel shows false 'sold out' and loses revenue. The reaper finds expired, still-<code>held</code> holds and releases their room-nights (<code>booked−1</code> per night) so they can be resold."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"medium",tag:"failure",q:"The reaper is down for an hour. What's the guest-visible symptom and how does the system self-heal?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"Your sweeper crashes and nobody notices for an hour."},
        {who:"cand",text:"Abandoned holds accumulate, so some hot nights show <strong>fewer rooms than truly available</strong> — a soft, revenue-losing failure, never a correctness one (it under-sells, it can't oversell). When the reaper restarts it simply scans the <code>expires_at</code> index and drains the backlog; release is idempotent, so catching up an hour late is fine."},
        {who:"cand",text:"To shrink the window I can add <strong>lazy expiry</strong>: at read/reserve time, a hold that's obviously past its TTL is treated as expired even before the sweep touches it. And I alarm on reaper lag (oldest un-swept expired hold) so an hour-long outage pages someone."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"hard",tag:"consistency",q:"A hold expires the same instant the guest's confirm arrives. Who wins, and why can't both fire?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"Confirm and expiry race on the same hold. I don't want the room both booked and released."},
        {who:"cand",text:"They contend on the <strong>hold row's lock</strong>, so one runs first. Confirm is <code>UPDATE holds SET status='confirmed' WHERE status='held' AND expires_at&gt;now()</code>; the reaper is <code>… SET status='expired' WHERE status='held' AND expires_at&lt;now()</code>. Both guard on <code>status='held'</code>, so whichever commits first flips the status and the other matches <strong>zero rows</strong> and no-ops."},
        {who:"cand",text:"If confirm wins, the booking is written and the reaper does nothing. If expiry wins, the room-nights are released and confirm fails cleanly — I void or refund the payment rather than hand the guest a room that was already resold. The row lock makes it strictly one outcome; there is no interleaving where the room is both confirmed and released."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
  ],
  holds:[
    {l:"easy",tag:"concept",q:"What is a hold and why does it carry a TTL?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"Define a hold."},
        {who:"cand",text:"A hold is a <strong>committed, time-limited reservation of the room-nights</strong> — the availability decrement made durable, with a <code>hold_id</code> and an <code>expires_at</code> ~10–15 min out. It gives the guest a window to pay while guaranteeing the room is theirs. The TTL bounds how long an abandoned checkout can strand inventory before the reaper releases it."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"medium",tag:"concept",q:"Why two-phase (hold then confirm) instead of a single book-and-pay call?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"Simpler to just decrement and charge in one transaction. Why not?"},
        {who:"cand",text:"Because payment takes seconds to minutes, and a book-and-pay transaction would hold the availability row's <strong>write lock for that entire time</strong>. On a hot night that serializes every other buyer behind one slow card — throughput collapses and timeouts cascade. Unacceptable under contention."},
        {who:"cand",text:"Two-phase decouples them: the hold does the atomic decrement and commits in milliseconds, releasing the lock immediately. Payment runs out of band against the <code>hold_id</code>, touching no availability lock. Confirm is then a cheap status flip. The room is protected by <em>data</em> (the decrement), not by a long-held lock."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"hard",tag:"consistency",q:"Show precisely how the idempotency key makes a retried reserve safe — transaction and all.",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"Give me the mechanism, not the hand-wave."},
        {who:"cand",text:"In one transaction: the conditional decrement, then <code>INSERT INTO holds(… , channel_id, idempotency_key, …) VALUES(…) ON CONFLICT (channel_id, idempotency_key) DO NOTHING RETURNING hold_id</code>, then COMMIT. The unique constraint on <code>(channel_id, idempotency_key)</code> is the guard."},
        {who:"cand",text:"First call: no conflict, the insert returns a <code>hold_id</code>, one decrement commits. Retry with the same key: the insert conflicts and returns nothing, so I detect the duplicate <em>inside the transaction</em>, skip/undo the decrement, and return the pre-existing hold. The DB's unique index is the serialization point, so even two truly-simultaneous retries can't both insert — one wins, one conflicts."},
        {who:"intv",text:"What if the decrement runs before you notice the conflict?"},
        {who:"cand",text:"I structure it so the hold insert is the gate: either do the insert first and only decrement on a successful insert, or run both and <code>ROLLBACK</code> the whole transaction on conflict, then read back and return the existing hold. Either way the net effect is exactly one decrement per key — the transaction guarantees the decrement and the insert live or die together."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"hard",tag:"failure",q:"A confirm lands one second after the hold expired and the room was already resold. What happens to the guest and the payment?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"The guest paid, but they lost the room to the reaper. Now what?"},
        {who:"cand",text:"Confirm's guard <code>status='held' AND expires_at&gt;now()</code> matches zero rows, so it does <strong>not</strong> write a booking. The system recognizes the hold is gone and treats the payment as a failed reservation: <strong>void the authorization</strong> if it was only authorized, or <strong>refund</strong> if captured. The guest is never charged for a room they don't have."},
        {who:"cand",text:"The UX response is to apologize and re-offer — the same dates if any room reopened, or alternates. The important invariant is that a late confirm can never override a completed release and double-sell the room; correctness is preserved and the money is made whole. To reduce how often this happens I keep the TTL generous enough for realistic payment times and only capture funds on a successful confirm."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
  ],
  sync:[
    {l:"easy",tag:"concept",q:"How do other channels learn that a room was just sold?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"Booking sells a room. How does Expedia find out?"},
        {who:"cand",text:"Through the <strong>channel manager</strong>. The confirm appended a <code>booking_events</code> row in the booking's transaction; the manager tails that outbox as a Kafka topic partitioned by <code>hotel_id</code> and pushes the new per-night counts to every channel's ARI. Within ~1–2s Expedia's search shows the room gone. Reserve is still the hard backstop if a channel is briefly behind."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"medium",tag:"design",q:"Pooled free-sell vs per-channel allotments — which do you pick and why?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"How do multiple channels share one hotel's rooms?"},
        {who:"cand",text:"Default is <strong>pooled free-sell</strong>: a single availability store, every channel reserves through the same atomic decrement. Cross-channel oversell is structurally impossible (one serialization point), and adding a channel needs no inventory carve-out. The trade-off is no channel is guaranteed rooms and everyone depends on the central service being online to sell."},
        {who:"cand",text:"<strong>Allotments</strong> carve a per-channel bucket per night so a channel can sell offline with guaranteed rooms, but they risk <em>stranded</em> inventory and reintroduce oversell if buckets overlap or a channel oversells its bucket — needing reconciliation and an overbooking cushion. I offer allotments as a per-hotel policy, but default to pooled because it's the safe, extensible choice."},
      ],
      resources:[]},
    {l:"hard",tag:"consistency",q:"Two channels sell the last room within the sync lag. Is that possible in your design? How do you resolve it?",
      turns:[
        {who:"intv",text:"The fan-out is ~1–2s behind. Can't two channels both sell the last room in that gap?"},
        {who:"cand",text:"In the <strong>pooled</strong> model, no. The sync lag only affects <em>search</em> freshness, not the reserve path — both channels' reserves funnel into the same atomic decrement on the same row, which serializes them. One wins, the other gets <code>409</code>. The stale cache might make both channels <em>show</em> the room, but only one can <em>hold</em> it. Display can be optimistic; the decrement cannot."},
        {who:"cand",text:"It's only possible under <strong>allotments</strong>, where each channel sells from its own bucket without a shared decrement. There a genuine cross-channel oversell can occur; I bound it with the <code>overbook_limit</code> cushion, detect it by reconciling <code>channel_allotment.sold</code> against confirmed bookings, and resolve it operationally — waitlist, rebook, or walk the guest with compensation. That risk is exactly the price of giving channels offline autonomy, which is why pooled is the default."},
      ],
      resources:[{title:"Kafka partitioning & ordering",url:"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#intro_concepts_and_terms"}]},
  ],
});
window.DATA['hotel'].mockTest = [
  {q:"What single modeling decision makes hotel reservation harder than a simple stock decrement, and how does it shape the design?",
    a:"A stay is an <strong>interval</strong>, not a single item: it spans multiple nights and needs a room on <em>every</em> night. So availability is modeled <strong>per night</strong> (a row per <code>(hotel, room_type, date)</code>), search takes <code>min(available)</code> over the range, and a reserve must decrement all nights <strong>atomically</strong> — all-or-nothing across the set, enforced by a rows-updated == nights check inside one transaction."},
  {q:"Give the exact mechanism that prevents double-booking the last room, and prove it's correct under concurrency.",
    a:"A single conditional statement in a single-shard transaction: <code>UPDATE availability SET booked=booked+1 WHERE …stay_date IN (nights) AND available≥1</code>, then assert <code>ROW_COUNT == nights</code> else <code>ROLLBACK</code>. Each night's row takes a write lock, so concurrent reserves <strong>serialize</strong>; exactly one commits the last room to <code>available=0</code>, and the others match zero rows on that night, fall short on the count, and roll back with a clean 409. No interleaving reads '1' twice."},
  {q:"Why decrement availability at hold time rather than at payment/confirm time?",
    a:"Because an advisory hold can't prevent oversell — two guests could both 'hold' the last room and both try to pay. The decrement must happen when the hold is placed. Confirm then only flips status and writes the booking; it's cheap because the room was already secured. This also keeps the expensive, slow payment step <strong>out of the availability row lock</strong>."},
  {q:"How do you keep the availability row lock from tanking throughput on a white-hot night during a sale?",
    a:"Keep the transaction minimal (lock → decrement → insert hold → commit; no payment inside), and put a <strong>serialization point in front of the hot row</strong> — a per-(hotel,room_type,date) queue/token bucket that hands out exactly <code>available</code> tokens so contenders line up in the app tier and hit the DB a bounded number of times. Losers get an instant 409 without touching the lock. Enable it dynamically only for detected hot rows."},
  {q:"Multiple channels sell the same rooms. How do you guarantee they never oversell each other, and how do their search views stay consistent?",
    a:"<strong>Pooled free-sell</strong>: one availability store, every channel's reserve goes through the same atomic decrement — a single serialization point, so cross-channel oversell is impossible. Search views converge via a <strong>transactional outbox</strong> → Kafka (partitioned by hotel_id) fan-out that pushes new counts to every channel's ARI within ~1–2s. Idempotent, versioned updates make redelivery and out-of-order messages safe."},
  {q:"How does a channel push in a booking it made on its own surface without opening an oversell hole?",
    a:"Through the <strong>same idempotent <code>/reserve</code></strong> as everyone else — no privileged back door. The idempotency key dedups retries, and the central atomic decrement means if the room is already gone the push-in gets a 409 and must waitlist/rebook. The central store stays the single source of truth; a channel can never bypass the decrement and silently oversell."},
  {q:"Walk the cancellation path. What changes, and how do other channels find out?",
    a:"A guarded <code>confirmed → cancelled</code> transition on the booking, a compensating <code>booked − 1</code> on each night of the stay (the reserve transaction in reverse), and a <code>cancelled</code> event appended to the outbox in the same transaction. The channel manager fans that out so every channel's ARI reopens the freed nights for resale. It's the same event-sourced machinery as reserve, run backward."},
  {q:"How do you support deliberate overbooking without special-casing the reserve path?",
    a:"One column. Availability is <code>available = total_rooms + overbook_limit − booked</code>, with <code>overbook_limit</code> set per <code>(hotel, room_type, date)</code> by revenue management. The reserve statement is unchanged (<code>WHERE available ≥ 1</code>), so overbooking is pure data/policy — no branch in the hot path. Realized cancellation rates feed back to tune the limit, and over-committed nights are flagged so a rare 'walk' can be handled operationally."},
];

